(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-nephrology-urinary-quiz-version-6"]={"title":"Nephrology & Urinary System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 6)","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The tubular pole of the renal corpuscle connects to which structure?","choices":["The efferent arteriole","The distal convoluted tubule","The afferent arteriole","The proximal convoluted tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The tubular pole of the renal corpuscle connects to the proximal convoluted tubule. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Within the juxtaglomerular apparatus, which cells specifically sense sodium (NaCl) and volume changes in the tubular fluid?","choices":["Podocytes","Macula densa cells","Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells","REPOS cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"The macula densa senses sodium (NaCl) and volume changes within the juxtaglomerular apparatus. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"The juxtaglomerular (granular) cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus are modified smooth muscle fibers derived from which structure?","choices":["The glomerular capillary","The afferent arteriole","The distal convoluted tubule","The efferent arteriole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are modified smooth muscle fibers of the afferent arteriole. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"The EPO-producing, oxygen-sensing cells of the kidney are known by which abbreviation in this lecture?","choices":["DCT cells","REPOS cells","PCT cells","JGA cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"The erythropoietin-producing, oxygen-sensing cells are abbreviated REPOS cells in this lecture. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"The ureter walls include an external connective-tissue adventitia, a muscularis, and a mucosa lined by which epithelium?","choices":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The mucosa of the ureter is lined by transitional epithelium, which gives it the ability to stretch. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"The muscularis of the ureter is arranged as inner longitudinal and outer circular smooth muscle, with an additional external longitudinal layer present in which part of the ureter?","choices":["Along its entire length","The middle third","The distal third","The proximal third"],"answer":2,"exp":"An additional external longitudinal muscle layer is present on the distal third of the ureter. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Parasympathetic innervation of the ureters is provided by which nerves?","choices":["Vagus nerve","Pudendal nerves","Greater splanchnic nerves","Pelvic splanchnic nerves"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ureters receive parasympathetic stimulation via the pelvic splanchnic nerves. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Sensory innervation of the ureters arises from which vertebral nerve roots?","choices":["T12–L2","L3–L5","T10–T11","T5–T9"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sensory innervation of the ureters arises from T12–L2 vertebral nerve roots. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"The muscular wall of the urinary bladder is composed of which named muscle?","choices":["Detrusor muscle","Bulbospongiosus","Trigone muscle","Levator ani"],"answer":0,"exp":"The muscular wall of the bladder is the detrusor muscle. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"The bladder can expand to hold approximately how much urine in the average adult?","choices":["100 mL","250 mL","1000 mL","500 mL"],"answer":3,"exp":"The bladder can expand roughly 15 times its empty volume to hold approximately 500 mL of urine in the average adult. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"The bladder wall's middle muscular (detrusor) layer is arranged as inner and outer longitudinal fibers surrounding which middle fiber orientation?","choices":["Oblique fibers","Circular fibers","Spiral fibers","Radial fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"The detrusor muscle's three layers are inner and outer longitudinal fibers surrounding middle circular fibers. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"In males, which structure is described as posterosuperior to the bladder?","choices":["The rectum","The seminal gland only","The pubic symphysis","The prostate gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"In males, the rectum is posterosuperior to the bladder. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"In males, which structure is described as inferior to the bladder?","choices":["The ureter itself","The seminal gland","The prostate gland","The rectum nearby"],"answer":2,"exp":"In males, the prostate gland is inferior to the bladder. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Venous drainage of the bladder proceeds via the vesical venous plexus to the internal iliac veins and then to the:","choices":["Portal vein","Inferior vena cava","External iliac vein","Renal vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Venous drainage of the bladder proceeds from the vesical venous plexus through the internal iliac veins to the inferior vena cava. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Innervation of the detrusor muscle for bladder contraction during urination is carried in part by which nerve?","choices":["The obturator nerve","The femoral nerve","The pudendal nerve","The hypogastric nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The detrusor muscle's innervation is described in the lecture as involving the hypogastric nerve. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"The average length of the female urethra is approximately:","choices":["3–4 cm","1–2 cm","8–10 cm","15 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The female urethra is approximately 3–4 cm long. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"The average length of the male urethra is approximately:","choices":["3–4 cm","18–20 cm","30 cm","8–10 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"The male urethra is approximately 18–20 cm long. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"The prostatic urethra is notable because multiple small prostatic ducts enter it during which process?","choices":["Defecation reflexes","Sexual stimulation","Normal micturition","Peristaltic waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Multiple small prostatic ducts enter the prostatic urethra during sexual stimulation. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"The membranous urethra pierces which structure on its course from the prostatic to the penile urethra?","choices":["The urogenital diaphragm","The rectus sheath","The inguinal canal","The pelvic diaphragm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The membranous urethra pierces the urogenital diaphragm. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"In females, the external urethral orifice is located anterior to the vaginal opening and posterior to which structure?","choices":["The mons pubis","The labia majora","The clitoris","The anus"],"answer":2,"exp":"In females, the external urethral orifice sits anterior to the vaginal opening and posterior to the clitoris. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"In males, the external urethral orifice is located at the end of which structure?","choices":["The membranous urethra","The penile (spongy) urethra","The ejaculatory duct","The prostatic urethra"],"answer":1,"exp":"In males, the external urethral orifice is located at the end of the penile (spongy) urethra. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"The external urethral sphincter, under voluntary control, is innervated by which nerve?","choices":["The hypogastric nerve","The vagus nerve","The pudendal nerve","The pelvic nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The external urethral sphincter is innervated by the pudendal nerve, providing voluntary control. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which four organs together make up the urinary system, per the lecture's introductory overview?","choices":["Kidneys, ureters, prostate, urethra","Kidneys, bladder, urethra, gonads","Kidneys, adrenal glands, ureters, bladder","Kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"The urinary system consists of the kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which vertebral disc level is used in the lecture's cross-sectional anatomy figure of the kidneys?","choices":["L1/L2","L3/L4","L4/L5","T12/L1"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture presents a cross section of the kidneys at the L1/L2 disc level. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which of the following is housed within the renal sinus, according to the lecture?","choices":["The renal pelvis and calyces","The adrenal gland above","The renal cortex only","The ureter's full length"],"answer":0,"exp":"The renal sinus houses the renal pelvis, renal calyces, blood/lymphatic vessels, nerves, and fat. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"NAVs (nerves, artery, and vein) enter and exit the kidney at which structure?","choices":["The renal hilum","The renal cortex","The renal papilla","The renal sinus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Nerves, the renal artery, and the renal vein (NAVs) enter and exit the kidney at the hilum, its concave medial border. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"The major calyces are formed by the convergence of which structures?","choices":["The renal columns","The renal pyramids directly","The minor calyces","The papillary ducts directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The major calyces are formed by the convergence of the minor calyces, which in turn drain individual renal papillae. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"During a percutaneous nephrolithotomy, a needle introduced through the abdominal wall into the renal pelvis passes through which sequence of layers?","choices":["Renal fascia, pararenal fat, perirenal fat, capsule of kidney","Pararenal fat, renal fascia, perirenal fat, capsule of kidney","Capsule of kidney, perirenal fat, renal fascia, pararenal fat","Perirenal fat, renal fascia, pararenal fat, capsule of kidney"],"answer":1,"exp":"From superficial to deep, a percutaneous nephrolithotomy needle passes through pararenal fat, renal fascia, perirenal fat, and finally the capsule of the kidney. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"On an intravenous pyelogram (contrast x-ray of the urinary system), the structure that collects urine from an individual renal papilla before it reaches a major calyx is the:","choices":["Renal pelvis","Minor calyx","Renal sinus","Renal column"],"answer":1,"exp":"The minor calyx collects urine draining from an individual renal papilla, before multiple minor calyces converge into a major calyx. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"The perforated surface of the renal papilla, through which papillary ducts open to release urine into the minor calyx, is called the:","choices":["Renal sinus cavity","Cribriform area","Renal column","Renal arch region"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cribriform area of the renal papilla is the perforated surface through which papillary ducts open into the minor calyx. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 14"}]};
